     \[
     \Delta \text{EHVI}_{1-0} = 0.8410 - 0.8409 = 0.0001
     \]
   - Option 1 also has **higher Entropy**:
     \[
     \Delta \text{Entropy}_{1-0} = 0.98 - 0.88 = 0.10
     \]
   - Per unit EHVI, Option 1 gives strictly more entropy; per unit entropy, it gives essentially the same EHVI. Formally, if we imagine a simple linear utility  
     \[
     U = \alpha \cdot \text{EHVI} + \beta \cdot \text{Entropy},
     \]
     then for any \(\beta > 0\), Option 1 dominates 0 because:  
     \[
     U_1 - U_0 = \alpha (0.0001) + \beta (0.10) > 0
     \]
     unless \(\beta\) is absurdly close to 0 relative to \(\alpha\). Given we are nowhere near the end of the campaign, \(\beta\) should not be negligible.

